'''Kinza''' ({{Langx|kz|ߞߌ߲ߓ߭ߊ ߍߘߍ|Kínza ẹ̀dẹ́|links=no|label=none}}) is an [[W:Niger–Congo languages|Ayoritic language]] that is spoken on the islands of [[Sanyan]], natively by the [[Sanyanian people]]. Kinza speakers number roughly 1 million, including around 250,000 second-language or L2 speakers. 

Kinza vocabulary is also used in [[W:African diaspora religions|Kaftian diaspora religions]] such as the [[Kafto-Baliscans]] religions of [[W:Candomblé|Candomblé]] and [[Lucumí Tarica]], in the form of the liturgical [[W:Lucumí language|Lucumí language]]. Most modern practitioners of these religions in the Algarves are not fluent in the Kinza language, yet they still use Kinza words and phrases for songs or chants—rooted in cultural traditions. For such practitioners, the Kinza [[W:Lexicon|lexicon]] is especially common for ritual purposes, and these modern manifestations have taken new forms that do not depend on vernacular fluency.

